+# Forking Base locally
+
+This checkout is the Foundry flavor of Scaffold-ETH 2. To run a local fork of Base:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork --network base
+```
+
+Then check Base-specific state, not just the local node's chain id:
+
+```bash
+BASE_USDC=0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+
+cast code $BASE_USDC --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+cast call $BASE_USDC 'symbol()(string)' --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+cast call $BASE_USDC 'decimals()(uint8)' --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+On a real Base fork, the code is not `0x`, `symbol()` returns `"USDC"`, and `decimals()` returns `6`.
+
+## What the old note got wrong
+
+The old note said to start the chain with:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork base
+```
+
+That does not select Base in this repo. The root script only delegates to the Foundry workspace:
+
+```json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork"
+```
+
+The Foundry workspace script then reads the second positional shell argument, falling back to `mainnet` when that argument is missing:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}"
+```
+
+With Yarn 4 script forwarding, the first token after the script name is `$0`, not `$1`. So in `yarn fork base`, `base` is `$0`, `$1` is empty, and the script silently uses the fallback:
+
+```bash
+make fork FORK_URL=mainnet
+```
+
+The Makefile passes that value straight to Anvil:
+
+```make
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+That is why reads from Base USDC came back empty: the local node was healthy, but it was forking Ethereum mainnet, where Base's USDC contract is not deployed at `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913`.
+
+## Why `cast chain-id` did not catch it
+
+`cast chain-id` only proves that the local JSON-RPC node is answering. It does not prove which upstream chain Anvil forked.
+
+The Makefile always starts the fork with:
+
+```make
+anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+So the local fork reports chain id `31337` whether the upstream is Base, Ethereum mainnet, or some other configured RPC endpoint. A useful fork check must read state that is specific to the target chain, such as bytecode or ERC-20 metadata at Base's canonical USDC address.
+
+This repo's Base RPC alias is defined in `packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+[rpc_endpoints]
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+Passing `--network base` makes `base` land in `$1`, so the workspace script expands to `make fork FORK_URL=base`, and Anvil resolves that alias from `foundry.toml`.

